# E3 session recordings: paper/results/sessions/ (see record_session.sh -- kept
# in-tree so they survive container restarts, which destroy anything in $HOME).
#
# The split is deliberate. The heavy binary payload stays on the operator's
# machine and is collected as files on request; everything textual is committed,
# because that is the half a reviewer actually reads and it is what makes a run
# attributable months later:
#
#   session.json        which cell, model, endpoint, provider, git commit
#   llm_chat/*.jsonl    LLM calls, guard firings, refusals -- metrics 4-7 and 9
#                       exist nowhere else, not in the bag and not in any topic
#   bag/metadata.yaml   rosbag2's own index: topics, message counts, duration.
#                       Small, and it answers "was this bag recorded correctly"
#                       without anyone fetching the bag.
#
# launch.log is NOT committed, despite being text: the blanket *.log rule above
# catches it and that is the right outcome here. A 20-robot bring-up with Nav2
# writes megabytes per run, so 61 of them is not "the light half" of a session.
# It ships with the bag when a run is collected as files.
#
# Git LFS was set up for the bags and then removed: a campaign is ~5 GB against
# a 1 GB free quota, and LFS objects keep consuming quota after deletion.
#
# sessions_invalid/ holds runs withdrawn from the set (wrong provider, a defect
# since fixed). Same split applies -- the reason a run was withdrawn is text and
# belongs in the repo, its 130 MB bag does not.
paper/results/sessions/**/*.db3
paper/results/sessions/**/*.db3-*
paper/results/sessions/**/*.mcap
paper/results/sessions/**/bag_record.log
paper/results/sessions_invalid/**/*.db3
paper/results/sessions_invalid/**/*.db3-*
paper/results/sessions_invalid/**/*.mcap
paper/results/sessions_invalid/**/bag_record.log
